WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.980
- No, I have to say I
was watching The Chair.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.330
And um, it was random.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.320
I just started watching the show.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.540
And this was a minute ago in my house.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.760
And all of sudden, your
whole thing start to come up.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.110
And they start describing you.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.360
And I had no idea like all
these things about you.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.158
I had no idea that you went to Princeton.

00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:15.943
I had no idea you were a novelist.

00:00:15.943 --> 00:00:17.370
I didn't know these things about you.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.003
I only knew you as a actor.

00:00:19.003 --> 00:00:20.250
- I like to keep it quiet.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.390
- I know, I'm like. But it's amazing.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.640
I literally googled. I'm
like is this for the show?

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.660
Or is this real?

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.890
And I googled you like
stalker, and you're amazing.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.902
- Well um, another thing
Amanda did- she went.

00:00:31.902 --> 00:00:34.950
I went to Princeton undergraduate,

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.770
and I wrote a senior thesis, a long paper

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.920
like a 100 page paper on
Samuel Beckett. And she went-

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.150
And you can get it from the library.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.280
You can't take it out. But
I guess you can xerox it.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.360
If anybody remembers what a
xerox is, uh back in the day.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.708
- I love a little kinos.

00:00:49.708 --> 00:00:50.699
(light laughing)

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:54.009
- So yeah, she xeroxed it.
It was on the set, you know?

00:00:54.009 --> 00:00:59.009
I hadn't seen it since I
handed it in 150 years ago.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.230
And I got to look at it
again, and I have to say,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.880
I didn't understand a word of it.

00:01:03.725 --> 00:01:04.974
(hysterical laughing)

00:01:04.974 --> 00:01:06.300
- I was very confusing.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.133
- So, they were reading your work?

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.010
- It was my work.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.333
- That's amazing.

00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:10.553
- Well, when Sandra Oh comes in,

00:01:10.553 --> 00:01:11.700
and she kinda throws it
on the table, that's it.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.340
That's the thing. That's my
paper. It was the actual thing.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.900
And I was just reading
it in-between takes.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.730
I was like, this is crap.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.428
- Oh, my gosh. We're all
hardest on ourselves.

00:01:20.428 --> 00:01:23.667
Well, someone tweeted
that they came across

00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:26.940
one of your old Yale textbooks.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.020
That's amazing.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.074
Like did they just find you that way?

00:01:30.074 --> 00:01:31.467
What did they just tweet you or something?

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:33.990
- No, I guess when I left New Haven,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.580
I guess I sold my books.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.590
Cause he brought that in a used bookstore.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.427
- You sell your books for money.

00:01:39.427 --> 00:01:41.080
You're not going to keep it, yeah.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.350
- I guess. That was a good
book. I wish I'd kept that one.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.183
- Oh.

00:01:44.183 --> 00:01:45.497
I'm sure they give it to you.

00:01:45.497 --> 00:01:46.330
(soft laugh)

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.720
